   "start_page": 344,
   "summary": "The report surveys Japanese foreign relations and domestic affairs during May 1937 under the Hayashi Cabinet. Foreign Minister Sato addressed proposals for a Pacific non-aggression pact, foreign oil companies received an aide-mémoire regarding Korean quotas, and Sino-Japanese friction intensified following agitation in North China, clashes in East Suiyuan, and the Swatow consular police incident. Diplomatic discussions continued with Great Britain and the Soviet Union, while Australia's Prime Minister Lyons proposed a Pacific treaty. Domestically, the Hayashi Cabinet suffered defeat in the April 30 general elections and resigned en bloc on May 31 amidst severe opposition from the Minseito, Seiyukai, and Prince Konoe, alongside an increasing trade deficit and military budgetary demands.",
