   "summary": "This despatch and monthly report detail Japanese internal and foreign affairs during June 1937, prominently featuring the formation of the Konoe Cabinet on June 4 following the resignation of Premier Hayashi. In relations with the United States, Japan formally rejected limiting battleship gun calibers to 14 inches without accompanying quantitative fleet limitations, and diplomatic assurances were exchanged regarding Japanese salmon fishing vessels in Alaskan waters and Bristol Bay. Anglo-Japanese discussions progressed toward examining concrete proposals, while severe armed clashes erupted between Soviet launches and Japanese-Manchurian ground forces over Kanchatzu and Chinamuho Islands on the Amur River. Domestically, the Konoe Cabinet established the Cabinet Planning Board under Foreign Minister Hirota, secured political party cooperation, and confronted an unfavorable trade balance exceeding 618 million yen for the first half of the year.",
