   "summary": "This despatch and monthly report record the major escalation of Sino-Japanese hostilities in North China following the Marco Polo Bridge (Lukouchiao) clash on July 7–8, 1937. Despite temporary local truces negotiated with General Sung Che-yuan of the 29th Army, clashes spread to Langfang, Tientsin, and Peiping, prompting the Japanese command to launch a general 'punitive campaign' and leading Chinese forces to withdraw toward Paoting. The United States and Great Britain communicated deep concern and offered good offices to compose differences while requesting protection for foreign nationals and treaty rights. Other sections note the peaceful resolution of the Soviet-Japanese Amur River dispute, the collapse of trade talks with the Netherlands Indies, the Diet's unanimous passage of supplementary military budgets totaling over 106 million yen, and rigorous revisions to Japanese foreign exchange control regulations.",
